## Steps

### Step 1: Add dot_product and normalize helpers to HrrVec

Add a `dot_product` method (skips norm computation) and `normalize_in_place` (mutates in place, avoids allocation). These are the building blocks for both LSH hashing and cached similarity.

```rust
// src/similarity/hrr.rs, impl HrrVec

pub fn dot_product(&self, other: &Self) -> f64 {
    self.data.iter().zip(&other.data).map(|(a, b)| a * b).sum()
}

pub fn normalize_in_place(&mut self) {
    let n = self.norm();
    if n < 1e-15 { return; }
    for x in &mut self.data {
        *x /= n;
    }
}
```

### Step 2: Implement SimHash LSH in duplicates.rs

Add a `SimHashIndex` struct that generates random hyperplanes and hashes vectors into buckets. Use L independent hash tables with k bits each for multi-probe coverage.

Parameters: k=10 bits per table, L=8 tables. At threshold 0.85 (angle ≈ 31.8°), the probability that a single hyperplane agrees is ~0.82. With k=10, P(collision in one table) ≈ 0.82^10 ≈ 0.137. With L=8 tables, P(at least one collision) = 1 - (1 - 0.137)^8 ≈ 0.69. That's too low for a hard guarantee.

Better: k=6, L=12. P(one table) = 0.82^6 ≈ 0.304. P(miss all 12) = 0.696^12 ≈ 0.012. False negative rate ~1.2% at threshold 0.85, improving rapidly at higher similarities. Good enough for near-duplicate detection where we only care about very high similarity.

Use a fixed seed (not random) for deterministic output — important for snapshot consistency.

```rust
struct SimHashIndex {
    tables: Vec<HashMap<u64, Vec<usize>>>,
    hyperplanes: Vec<Vec<Vec<f64>>>,  // [table][bit][dim]
}

impl SimHashIndex {
    fn new(dim: usize, k: usize, num_tables: usize, seed: u64) -> Self {
        let mut rng = Rng::new(seed);
        let hyperplanes: Vec<Vec<Vec<f64>>> = (0..num_tables)
            .map(|_| {
                (0..k)
                    .map(|_| (0..dim).map(|_| rng.next_gaussian()).collect())
                    .collect()
            })
            .collect();
        Self {
            tables: vec![HashMap::new(); num_tables],
            hyperplanes,
        }
    }

    fn hash_vector(&self, vec: &[f64], table_idx: usize) -> u64 {
        let mut hash = 0u64;
        for (bit, plane) in self.hyperplanes[table_idx].iter().enumerate() {
            let dot: f64 = vec.iter().zip(plane).map(|(a, b)| a * b).sum();
            if dot >= 0.0 {
                hash |= 1 << bit;
            }
        }
        hash
    }

    fn insert(&mut self, idx: usize, vec: &[f64]) {
        for t in 0..self.tables.len() {
            let h = self.hash_vector(vec, t);
            self.tables[t].entry(h).or_default().push(idx);
        }
    }

    fn candidate_pairs(&self) -> HashSet<(usize, usize)> {
        let mut pairs = HashSet::new();
        for table in &self.tables {
            for bucket in table.values() {
                for i in 0..bucket.len() {
                    for j in (i+1)..bucket.len() {
                        let (a, b) = if bucket[i] < bucket[j] {
                            (bucket[i], bucket[j])
                        } else {
                            (bucket[j], bucket[i])
                        };
                        pairs.insert((a, b));
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        pairs
    }
}
```

### Step 3: Add similarity cache

A simple wrapper around `HashMap<(usize, usize), f64>` with a canonical key ordering.

```rust
struct SimCache {
    cache: HashMap<(usize, usize), f64>,
}

impl SimCache {
    fn new() -> Self { Self { cache: HashMap::new() } }

    fn get_or_compute(&mut self, i: usize, j: usize, vecs: &[(i64, HrrVec)]) -> f64 {
        let key = if i < j { (i, j) } else { (j, i) };
        *self.cache.entry(key).or_insert_with(|| {
            // vectors already normalized, so dot product = cosine similarity
            vecs[key.0].1.dot_product(&vecs[key.1].1)
        })
    }
}
```

### Step 4: Rewrite find_pattern_families to use LSH + cache

Replace the brute-force loop with: pre-normalize → LSH index → candidate pairs → exact verify → union-find → complete-link pruning (using cache) → avg_similarity (using cache).

```rust
pub fn find_pattern_families(
    vectors: &[(i64, HrrVec)],
    threshold: f64,
    min_group: usize,
) -> Vec<PatternFamily> {
    let n = vectors.len();
    if n < min_group {
        return Vec::new();
    }

    // Pre-normalize
    let normalized: Vec<(i64, HrrVec)> = vectors.iter()
        .map(|(id, v)| (*id, v.normalize()))
        .collect();

    // LSH candidate generation
    let dim = normalized[0].1.data.len();
    let mut index = SimHashIndex::new(dim, 6, 12, 0xDEAD_BEEF);
    for (i, (_, v)) in normalized.iter().enumerate() {
        index.insert(i, &v.data);
    }
    let candidates = index.candidate_pairs();

    // Exact verify + union-find
    let mut cache = SimCache::new();
    let mut uf = UnionFind::new(n);
    for (i, j) in &candidates {
        let sim = cache.get_or_compute(*i, *j, &normalized);
        if sim >= threshold {
            uf.union(*i, *j);
        }
    }

    // Group by component, complete-link prune, compute avg — all using cache
    // (same logic as current Phases 2 and 3, but calling cache.get_or_compute
    //  instead of vectors[a].1.cosine_similarity(&vectors[b].1))
    ...
}
```

